WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.803
Four students, Ana, Ben, Cai and Dev

2
00:00:06.803 --> 00:00:10.179
come to a room with five seats

3
00:00:10.179 --> 00:00:12.500
numbered one to five.

4
00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:17.121
Here is a seating plan, drawn as arrows.

5
00:00:17.121 --> 00:00:19.470
Ana sits in seat two.

6
00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.392
Ben in seat five.

7
00:00:21.392 --> 00:00:26.090
Cai also in seat two, and Dev in seat four.

8
00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.368
Is any seat shared?

9
00:00:28.368 --> 00:00:30.296
Is any seat empty?

10
00:00:30.296 --> 00:00:31.920
Pause and look.

11
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:37.670
Seat two gets two arrows

12
00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.580
Ana and Cai share it.

13
00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:42.184
Seats one and three get none

14
00:00:42.184 --> 00:00:43.660
they stay empty.

15
00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:45.737
But one thing is right.

16
00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:47.689
Every student has exactly

17
00:00:47.764 --> 00:00:49.491
one arrow leaving them.

18
00:00:49.566 --> 00:00:51.593
That one rule is what makes

19
00:00:51.668 --> 00:00:53.170
the plan a function.

20
00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:57.073
Write the plan as pairs: Ana with two

21
00:00:57.073 --> 00:00:59.347
Ben with five, and so on.

22
00:00:59.347 --> 00:01:01.971
A set of pairs is a relation

23
00:01:01.971 --> 00:01:04.070
from students to seats.

24
00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:07.825
A relation f is a function when no

25
00:01:07.925 --> 00:01:11.030
first element has two partners.

26
00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:14.836
If a comes with b one and with b two

27
00:01:14.836 --> 00:01:17.340
then b one equals b two.

28
00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:21.300
And f from A to B also asks that

29
00:01:21.413 --> 00:01:24.459
every element of A appears

30
00:01:24.459 --> 00:01:27.730
the domain of f is all of A.

31
00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.209
Two plans that fail.

32
00:01:30.209 --> 00:01:33.437
Give Ben a second seat, seat one.

33
00:01:33.437 --> 00:01:35.906
Now Ben has two partners

34
00:01:35.906 --> 00:01:38.470
so this is not a function.

35
00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.741
Take that arrow away again

36
00:01:40.741 --> 00:01:43.160
and take away Dev's seat instead.

37
00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:46.077
Each student still has at most one seat

38
00:01:46.077 --> 00:01:47.358
but Dev has none.

39
00:01:47.358 --> 00:01:50.418
The domain is no longer all four students

40
00:01:50.489 --> 00:01:52.908
so this is not a function from the

41
00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:54.900
four students to the seats.

42
00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:58.904
A function needs both: every student

43
00:01:58.904 --> 00:02:01.570
and exactly one seat each.

44
00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:05.639
The seats the plan actually reaches, two

45
00:02:05.639 --> 00:02:08.703
four and five, form the image of f.

46
00:02:08.703 --> 00:02:11.000
The set of all five seats

47
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.830
where f is allowed to land, is the codomain.

48
00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.115
How many plans are there?

49
00:02:17.192 --> 00:02:19.127
Each of the four students

50
00:02:19.204 --> 00:02:21.062
picks one of five seats

51
00:02:21.062 --> 00:02:22.842
so five to the fourth

52
00:02:22.842 --> 00:02:25.860
six hundred and twenty-five functions.

53
00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:30.869
A function is one-to-one when f of a equals

54
00:02:30.972 --> 00:02:34.885
f of a prime only if a equals a prime.

55
00:02:34.885 --> 00:02:39.520
In the picture: no seat receives two arrows.

56
00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:42.475
Our plan fails at seat two

57
00:02:42.475 --> 00:02:44.790
where Ana and Cai meet.

58
00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:49.351
Move Ana to seat three and Cai to seat one.

59
00:02:49.351 --> 00:02:51.506
Call this plan P two.

60
00:02:51.506 --> 00:02:54.443
Every student now sits alone

61
00:02:54.443 --> 00:02:56.500
P two is one-to-one.

62
00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:01.140
A function is onto when every element

63
00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:04.871
of B receives at least one arrow.

64
00:03:04.871 --> 00:03:09.480
P two is not onto: seat two is empty now.

65
00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:12.845
In fact no plan for four students

66
00:03:12.937 --> 00:03:15.038
can be onto five seats

67
00:03:15.038 --> 00:03:18.510
four arrows reach at most four seats.

68
00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:21.739
Now a function on numbers

69
00:03:21.739 --> 00:03:25.000
f of n equals three n plus two

70
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:28.566
from the integers to the integers.

71
00:03:28.566 --> 00:03:32.845
Minus one goes to minus one, zero to two

72
00:03:32.845 --> 00:03:34.170
one to five.

73
00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:39.087
One-to-one: suppose three n plus two equals

74
00:03:39.193 --> 00:03:41.636
three n prime plus two.

75
00:03:41.636 --> 00:03:44.928
Subtract two, divide by three

76
00:03:44.928 --> 00:03:46.840
n equals n prime.

77
00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:47.733
Onto?

78
00:03:47.733 --> 00:03:49.794
Try to reach zero.

79
00:03:49.902 --> 00:03:53.591
Three n plus two equals zero gives

80
00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.521
n equals minus two thirds

81
00:03:56.521 --> 00:03:59.233
which is not an integer.

82
00:03:59.233 --> 00:04:03.790
So zero gets no arrow, and f is not onto.

83
00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:05.762
A common mix-up

84
00:04:05.849 --> 00:04:08.373
one-to-one does not mean each

85
00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:10.287
input has one output.

86
00:04:10.287 --> 00:04:14.116
Take g of n equals the absolute value of n

87
00:04:14.116 --> 00:04:17.770
from the integers to the natural numbers.

88
00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:21.310
Each input has exactly one output

89
00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:23.468
as for every function.

90
00:04:23.468 --> 00:04:27.033
But two and minus two both go to two

91
00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:30.880
two arrows meet, so g is not one-to-one.

92
00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:32.962
It is onto, though

93
00:04:33.054 --> 00:04:35.333
every natural number m is

94
00:04:35.424 --> 00:04:37.430
the image of m itself.

95
00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:40.695
A function that is one-to-one

96
00:04:40.788 --> 00:04:43.010
and onto is a bijection.

97
00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:47.177
Back to the room, with a fifth student, Eli.

98
00:04:47.177 --> 00:04:51.159
Five students, five seats, no seat shared

99
00:04:51.159 --> 00:04:54.770
every seat receives exactly one arrow.

100
00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:57.254
The rule three x plus two

101
00:04:57.340 --> 00:04:58.962
is a bijection too

102
00:04:58.962 --> 00:05:02.120
from the rationals to the rationals.

103
00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:06.675
Given any rational y, solve for x

104
00:05:06.675 --> 00:05:09.766
y minus two, over three.

105
00:05:09.766 --> 00:05:15.208
That x is rational, so it is in the domain

106
00:05:15.208 --> 00:05:17.310
and f of x is y.

107
00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:21.497
So: a function sends every input

108
00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:24.316
along exactly one arrow.

109
00:05:24.316 --> 00:05:27.585
One-to-one, no output twice.

110
00:05:27.585 --> 00:05:30.290
Onto, no output missed.

111
00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:35.070
Try it: is f of n equals five minus two

112
00:05:35.183 --> 00:05:38.477
n one-to-one on the integers?

113
00:05:38.477 --> 00:05:39.840
Is it onto?

114
00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:43.307
In part two we turn the arrows round

115
00:05:43.307 --> 00:05:45.834
chain two functions together

116
00:05:45.834 --> 00:05:48.950
and put seven students in six seats.
